"The letters Kaf, Ha, Ya, Ayn, Sad at the beginning of the surah are allegorical (mutashabih), and their definitive meaning is known only to Allah. Although there are narrations suggesting that these letters point to the names of Allah or to individuals mentioned in the surah, they are not sound. These letters, from which no literal dictionary meaning can be derived linguistically, are open to countless possibilities through rational deduction; however, their primary benefit is to demonstrate the incapacity of the human intellect to comprehend lofty objectives.\n\nIn the story of Mary, Gabriel appears in human form to Mary, who had withdrawn from her family to an eastern place. Mary seeks refuge in Allah, while the angel tells her that he is a messenger from her Lord to bestow upon her a pure boy. When Mary states that no human has ever touched her, the angel informs her that this is easy for Allah, and that the child will be a sign (miracle) and a mercy for mankind. Mary becomes pregnant, and with the pains of childbirth, she clings to the trunk of a date palm; the angel tells her not to grieve, that a stream flows beneath her, that ripe dates will fall when she shakes the palm, that she should eat, drink, and be comforted, and that since she has vowed a fast, she should speak to no one.\n\nWhen Mary brings Jesus to her people, they accuse her; she then points to Jesus in the cradle. Jesus speaks, saying that he is a servant of Allah, that He has given him the Scripture and made him a prophet, commanded him prayer and zakat, made him dutiful to his mother, and that peace was upon him the day he was born, the day he dies, and the day he will be raised alive. These words emphasize that Jesus is merely a servant and that it is unthinkable for Allah to take a child. The factions (Jews and Christians) fell into dispute concerning Jesus and became disbelievers; they will regret it on the Day of Resurrection. Allah will inherit the entire earth, and everyone will be returned to Him.\n\nIn the story of Abraham, he tells his father not to worship idols, not to follow Satan, and that he fears the punishment of Allah. When his father threatens to stone him, Abraham bids him peace and seeks forgiveness for him, then withdraws from everything besides Allah and supplicates to his Lord. Thereupon, Allah bestows upon him Isaac and Jacob as prophets. Then the story of Moses is recounted: Moses was a sincere servant who was called from the right side of Mount Sinai (Tur), and Aaron was granted to him as a prophet. Ishmael was a prophet true to his promise; he used to enjoin prayer and zakat upon his family. Idris was a most truthful prophet, and Allah raised him to a high station.\n\nThese prophets were the distinguished ones whom Allah blessed with favor. After them came a generation that neglected prayer and pursued their lusts; they will be cast into the Valley of Ghayya. However, those who repent, believe, and perform righteous deeds will enter Paradise and will not be wronged. This Paradise consists of the Gardens of Eden ('Adn) that will be given to the righteous. Following this, it is reported that Gabriel said to the Prophet (peace be upon him): 'We descend only by the command of your Lord; to Him belongs whatever is before us, whatever is behind us, and whatever is in between; and your Lord has never been forgetful.' This verse is an answer to a question seeking the reason for Gabriel's descent.\n\nMan denies that he will be resurrected after death, whereas Allah created him from nothing. Allah will gather the disbelievers along with their devils on the Plain of Resurrection, then bring them to their knees around Hell, and separate the most rebellious from each group. Everyone will approach Hell, after which the God-fearing will be saved, while the wrongdoers will remain in Hell. The disbelievers mock the believers, claiming that they are superior in status and company. Yet Allah increases guidance for those who are guided; righteous deeds are better in reward and outcome. The case of al-'As ibn Wa'il is described, who denied the verses and said, 'I will surely be given wealth and children'; he had told his creditor Khabbab that he would pay him with his wealth once resurrected after death. Allah will record what he says, strip him of his wealth, and increase his punishment. In the end, the God-fearing will be gathered before the Most Merciful (al-Rahman), the criminals will be driven thirsty into Hell, and only those who have taken a covenant will be able to intercede. Those who worship others besides Allah will regret it, and on the Day of Resurrection, each person will come before Him alone. The Most Merciful will bestow affection upon those who believe and do righteous deeds; the Quran has been made easy to give glad tidings and warnings. It is then recalled that earlier generations were destroyed."